Round 21 (2026-02-27)

What landed
- Files changed:
  - `clang/aot/_.c`
- Goal of this round: remove a hidden interpreter-only path inside compiled strict forcing.

Root cause found
- `aot_force_whnf_local(term, stack_top)` temporarily set `AOT_TRY_CALL_ENABLED = 0` before calling `wnf(term)`.
- This meant strict forcing used by compiled `SWI/MAT` scrutinees (and DUP-force retries) could never dispatch to compiled refs, even when the scrutinee was an APP/REF tree that is AOT-compilable.
- Effect: lots of size-2 heap traffic came from interpreter-style forcing inside otherwise compiled execution, with no `AOT_DEOPT` signal (so it looked like “fully compiled” while still paying fallback-like costs).

Code change
1) Allow compiled dispatch during local strict forcing
- In `aot_force_whnf_local`:
  - removed temporary disable/restore of `AOT_TRY_CALL_ENABLED`
  - kept `WNF_S_POS` save/restore behavior unchanged
- Net: `wnf()` invoked by strict forcing can now use `aot_try_call` normally.

Why this is safe (observed)
- `aot_try_call` already has depth guards (`AOT_TRY_CALL_MAX_DEPTH`, `AOT_MAX_DEPTH`) and step-limit guard (`STEPS_ITRS_LIM`).
- `aot_force_whnf_local` still isolates stack segment via `WNF_S_POS = stack_top` and restore, preserving caller frame ownership boundaries.
- Full test suite passes.

